y0rIeD","description":"Drawdown of Winchester Dam to patch holes in this ailing high-hazard structure.","caption":"Winchester Dam Maintenance","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"dyi8mXwRp4WHpnWfMYeYu","photo_date":"2023-08-19T17:51:21.000Z","updated_at":"2023-08-20T17:51:22.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/FFaVBw2H7oK2YVQNdv8Xy","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/FFaVBw2H7oK2YVQNdv8Xy"}},{"id":"890856","ext":"jpg","post_id":"894780","description":null,"caption":"Dixon Falls","poi_name":"Dixon Falls","subject":null,"author":"Sam Morrison","poi_id":"107045","photo_date":"1900-01-01T00:22:52.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T00:22:52.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/890856","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/890856"}},{"id":"890860","ext":"jpg","post_id":"894780","description":null,"caption":" on Umpqua, N. @2. Idleyld Park to confluence with S. Umpqua","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":"1900-01-01T00:22:52.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T00:22:52.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/890860","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/890860"}},{"id":"VQYatzz8dxhzrzAQv7OrW","ext":"jpg","post_id":"yJwAhPsqR48AUKcy0rIeD","description":"Drawdown of Winchester Dam to patch holes in this ailing high-hazard structure.","caption":"Winchester Dam Maintenance","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"dyi8mXwRp4WHpnWfMYeYu","photo_date":"2023-08-19T17:51:21.000Z","updated_at":"2023-08-20T17:51:22.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/VQYatzz8dxhzrzAQv7OrW","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/VQYatzz8dxhzrzAQv7OrW"}}],"detail":{"id":"1562","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"This is a great section for open canoeists, beginner kayakers/rafters, and drift boats. The river maintains gradient of about 11 fpm providing lots of class II and a few class IIIs for novice boaters. The river flows by grassy green hills with oak forests interspersed by farms, ranches, and private homes.\n\nApproximately 2 miles downstream of Idleyld Park, Lone Rock Park near Glide, OR has a boat slide along Lone Park Road on the south side of the river. Drift boats use the steep ramp and the small dirt parking area here. A few miles downstream from here is Colliding Rivers, the most interesting rapid on the run. Two channels provide options with the left channel being visible from the National Forest turnout in Glide. Drift boats and novice paddlers prefer the left channel to the steeper drop on the right.\n\nMore class II continues interspersed by sections of calm water until Whistlers Bend. Here the river turns north into a class III set of standing waves at higher flows and a more technical rapid at lower flows. As the river comes back around the bend to the south, Whistlers Bend County Park provides an option to camp (sites 9 and 11 are next to the river) and make an overnight trip on this section\n\nThe next class III is Dixon Falls on the right side of an island about 4 miles downstream from Whistlers Bend. At higher flows around 5000cfs a large wave hole forms with a straightforward line down the channel. Beyond Dixon Falls paddlers encounter fewer private homes and the river has a bit more of a wilderness feel. Class II continues to the river left take-out about 1 mile before the town of Winchester, OR. Be sure to find the small dirt ramp access site beforehand as downstream lies the Winchester Dam and fish ladder which is a hazard to navigation.\n\nBelow the dam access is available at John P Amacher Park. From this point the river continues for 6.6 miles of class II to the confluence with the South Umpqua.\n\nWhile this section can be run very high in the spring, at flows above 3000-4000 cfs, the current becomes stronger and eddies less frequent, making it less ideal for beginner kayakers while still a nice float trip for rafts.","averageGradient":"10","maxGradient":null,"length":"32","correlations":[{"reachID":"1562","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"1000","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"100000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"NORTH UMPQUA RIVER AT WINCHESTER, OR","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"14319500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"1500","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"2.6","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"43.27096667","longitude":"-123.4115444"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/14319500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/14319500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"migration-runnable","latestReading":{"value":"1500","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["109909","107043","386ULdqxW4etdFodai6Kn","uRKhCFrY23JwNTILvdD7s","7Wnyi8u6RUV91jMCuAf7q","dyi8mXwRp4WHpnWfMYeYu","2Xcbjye36GoyM2sdXc00g","zFaLIUjmWb9Ouv4ADXbHY","115161"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-123.061808,43.316878],[-123.0627,43.31624],[-123.0627,43.31624],[-123.063043,43.316045],[-123.063043,43.316045],[-123.065565,43.314289],[-123.065565,43.314289],[-123.067592,43.31261],[-123.067592,43.31261],[-123.070081,43.3105